From: Angelpro09xd <167892325+Angelpro09xd@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] sapphire-0246: AppleKeyStore passcode state machine (real lock, verified passcode)

SweetKeyStore keeps a real passcode without any kernel file I/O: a salted
PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 verifier (20000 rounds) lives in the system keybag blob
that keybagd saves (aks_save_bag, 3) as KeyBagKeys in
/private/var/keybags/systembag.kb and loads back at boot: aks_get_system (14)
answers kIOReturnNotFound once, to keybagd --init, whose next aks_load_bag (6)
is the system bag (a foreign blob such as the image's "SWEET" placeholder is a
bag without a passcode). With a passcode the device boots locked before first
unlock, locks on aks_lock_device (8/32), unlocks only with the right passcode
(9, 42), changes/removes it through keybagd's aks_change_secret path
(15, 3, 5, 105, 4), counts failures with iOS back-off, reports MKB lock state
3 without a passcode, and sends the kext's IOGeneralInterest messages
(0xe3ffd00d lock state, 0xe3ffd00f first unlock) with no lock held. No lock is
held across the KDF, logging, messageClients or thread calls.
Key wrap/unwrap (10/11) unchanged. Boot-args: sweetaks_nopass=1 (ignore the
stored passcode), sweetaks_grace=N.

See research/passcode-aks.md.
---
diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetKeyStore.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetKeyStore.cpp
index 66de894..c30fe0a 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetKeyStore.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetKeyStore.cpp
@@ -1,18 +1,49 @@
 /*
- * Built-in AppleKeyStore for SWEET: a device without a passcode whose
- * system keybag is always loaded and unlocked.
+ * Built-in AppleKeyStore for SWEET: the device passcode state machine
+ * without a Secure Enclave.
  *
  * Apple's AppleKeyStore kext (closed, SEP-backed) owns the keybags behind
- * data protection. libaks (AppleKeyStore.framework, statically in keybagd)
- * opens IOServiceMatching("AppleKeyStore") with type 0, calls selector 0,
- * then one selector per aks_* function (iOS 16.7 numbering below). keybagd
- * --init asks for the system bag (14) and only loads systembag.kb when the
- * answer is kIOReturnNotFound; answering with a handle is the path a
- * userspace reboot takes, so no bag file is needed.
+ * data protection. libaks (AppleKeyStore.framework, statically in keybagd and
+ * in MobileKeyBag.framework) opens IOServiceMatching("AppleKeyStore") with
+ * type 0, calls selector 0, then one selector per aks_* function (iOS 16.7
+ * numbering below).
+ *
+ * There is no data-protection encryption on this port (HFS+ root without
+ * cprotect), so nothing here protects data. What it keeps correctly is the
+ * lock/passcode behaviour the system expects:
+ *  - the passcode (set in Settings: profiled -> keybagd -> aks_change_secret)
+ *    is kept as a salted PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 verifier, never the passcode, in
+ *    the system keybag blob. This kernel does no file I/O: keybagd stores the
+ *    blob we hand out from aks_save_bag (3) as KeyBagKeys in
+ *    /private/var/keybags/systembag.kb (plain plist: the ADT has
+ *    /defaults/no-effaceable-storage), and hands it back at boot:
+ *    keybagd --init load_devicebag asks aks_get_system (14), and only on
+ *    kIOReturnNotFound reads systembag.kb, aks_load_bag (6), aks_set_system (5),
+ *    aks_unload_bag (4), then aks_get_system again (must succeed: any failure
+ *    there is keybagd's FATAL KEYBAG ERROR, reboot into recovery). So 14 says
+ *    NotFound exactly once, to keybagd, and the next keybagd load (6) is the
+ *    system bag. A blob that is not ours (the image's "SWEET" placeholder) is
+ *    accepted as a bag without a passcode;
+ *  - with a passcode the device boots locked (before first unlock), locks on
+ *    aks_lock_device (SpringBoard, MKBLockDevice), and unlocks only with the
+ *    right passcode (aks_unlock_device from SpringBoard / profiled,
+ *    aks_verify_password); wrong ones are counted and backed off like iOS
+ *    (the count reaches the disk with the next bag save, otherwise it lives
+ *    until reboot);
+ *  - every lock state change is announced the way the kext does it, with an
+ *    IOGeneralInterest message (MobileKeyBag MKBEventsRegister, UserEventAgent
+ *    MobileKeyBagLockState -> com.apple.mobile.keybagd.lock_status/first_unlock),
+ *    sent with no lock held.
+ * Without a stored verifier the device reports "no passcode" (MKB lock state 3).
+ *
+ * Boot-args: sweetaks_nopass=1 ignores the stored passcode (recovery when
+ * locked out; the next bag save drops it), sweetaks_grace=N seconds of grace
+ * after a normal lock (default 0, iOS's "Require passcode immediately").
  *
  * Every call is logged (selector, caller, argument shapes, result) so the
  * next selectors the system needs show up on the console; unknown ones fail
- * with kIOReturnUnsupported.
+ * with kIOReturnUnsupported. Passcodes and verifiers are never logged, and
+ * nothing is logged with a lock held.
  */
 #include <pexpert/arm64/board_config.h>
 
@@ -21,43 +52,83 @@
